{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And the earth brought forth, etc.</b> After the verse said ״וַיְהִי כֵן״, it should not have needed to say this entire verse. Rather, the reason is to make us hear two novel matters — one concerning the deshe and one concerning the tree. One concerning the deshe, that they drew a kal va-chomer and went out לְמִינֵהֶם. And one needs to know for what purpose the verse informed us that they did not emerge mixed together. Perhaps it comes to inform us that it is forbidden to graft one species of deshe with another, as Rabbinah raised in tractate Ḥullin (60a), and this is their language: ״One who grafts this species of deshe onto this species — for Rabbi Ḥanina bar Pappa, what is the ruling? Since לְמִינֵהוּ is not written concerning them, etc. — or perhaps, since the Holy One, blessed be He, agreed with them, it is as though לְמִינֵהוּ is written concerning them — תֵּיקוּ,״ end quote. And Rashi explained in the passage beginning ״for Rabbi Ḥanina,״ and this is his language: ״That means of their own understanding they went out לְמִינֵהֶם and they were not commanded concerning this,״ end quote. It implies from his words that the Gemara holds that from the unexpressed implication they were commanded concerning this; and even though it was not mentioned explicitly next to the deshe, what the verse says ״לְמִינוֹ״ at the end refers back to the tree and to the deshe. Now according to the words of Rabbi Ḥanina bar Pappa — that HaShem did not say ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ except concerning the tree, and the deshe drew a kal va-chomer — the verse, by returning to say ״וַתּוֹצֵא הָאָרֶץ,״ made us hear that the deshe drew the kal va-chomer. And according to what the Gemara holds — if not for the words of Rabbi Ḥanina bar Pappa, who said that ״לְמִינוֹ״ also refers back to the deshe — it appears that it makes us hear that the deshe understood the matter in its full clarity, that what He said ״לְמִינוֹ״ is also said concerning the deshe, and this was not understood by us from the first verse. And even though it said in the first verse ״וַיְהִי כֵן,״ nevertheless the meaning of ״לְמִינוֹ״ is not understood — whether it refers back to everything said in the verse — and the second verse came and determined that ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ refers back to everything stated in the verse. And from here you learn that in every place where HaShem speaks in the order of the matters said in this context, the meaning of His words is as the deshe acted." "<b>And He made us know</b> the verse through the act of the bringing forth of the tree, that the earth did not fulfill the statement of the Creator, which was that He said to it ״עֵץ פְּרִי״ — that the tree itself should be of the taste of the fruit — and it brought forth ״עֵץ עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי״, and not so the tree itself." "<b>And to this</b> I will look — how could the earth rebel against the One who spread out the earth, all the more so since it has no evil inclination, for the Sages did not establish (Berakhot 61a) that it has an evil inclination except for the beast, from its kicking, etc. — and we explained them in their place (in the book Chefetz HaShem) that because it needs the sense of desire in order to become impassioned, the Holy One, blessed be He, made for it a heating inclination, but the earth — we find no evidence that it has an evil inclination that would incite it to rebel with rebellion to transgress the word of HaShem." "<b>Further,</b> one must investigate this, for we do not find it mentioned in the Creation that HaShem said the earth should bring forth barren trees for the use of wood, and we see that the category of trees that are in the world has no end — they are more numerous than fruit trees. And therefore it is possible to say that before the curse of the earth that HaShem cursed, all the trees bore fruit; but after He cursed the ground, its strength was diminished and not all the trees bore fruit, but thorns and thistles." "<b>However,</b> the explanation of the matter is that the earth was precise with the wisdom of HaShem that He founded in her, as it is written (Proverbs 3:19) ״בְּחָכְמָה יָסַד אָרֶץ״. She scrutinized the statement of HaShem to her, that she bring forth עֵץ פְּרִי עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי, which bears two ways: the first, that the tree be of the taste of its fruit — in which case the intention would be that the tree itself be fruit and also produce fruit. And from His saying ״עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי״ she explained another way — that the intention is on this path: עֵץ פְּרִי by itself and עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי by itself, meaning a tree that produces fruit alone from the outset, and they are two kinds. And the reason He did not say ״וְעֹשֶׂה פְּרִי״ with a vav, so as not to be understood that the tree itself should be fruit and additionally produce fruit. Also, in the meaning of ״עֵץ פְּרִי״ there are two ways: the first, a tree that is itself fruit; and the second, one that is fit to produce fruit but does not produce fruit. And the earth was cunning and brought forth three kinds that are hearable in the words of HaShem, due to her great zeal: the first — they are trees that produce fruit and the taste of the wood is not like the taste of their fruit; and the second — that produce fruit and the taste of their wood is like the taste of their fruit, and that is the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, concerning which they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah, chapter 15) that the taste of its wood was like the taste of its fruit; and the third — it is a tree that resembles עֵץ פְּרִי, and they are barren trees — and behold, she was zealous to do everything that could be heard. And that is what the verse says: <b>וַתּוֹצֵא הָאָרֶץ דֶּשֶׁא</b> etc. <b>וְעֵץ עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי</b> — it was precise to say <b>וְעֵץ</b> with an additional vav for no need, just as the verse likewise spoke in the preceding divine statement, where it was not necessary to say ״וְעֵץ״ with an additional vav. The verse intended to say: besides the other trees that it brought forth, it further brought forth עֵץ עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי. And here the verse includes עֵץ פְּרִי, concerning which they of blessed memory said that the Tree of Knowledge had the taste of its wood like the taste of its fruit. And it is possible that it also includes trees that do not produce fruit that exist in the world, when we say that they existed before the curse of the ground. And the reason that HaShem cursed the ground — according to the one who says it was because it altered — I will explain in its place, with the help of Heaven."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:55:34.304402140Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}